 capabilities they're  fairly weak there now but they want to  rebuild their capabilities and what this  does more than anything else is says to  them that we have to worry first and  foremost about our security even in  taliban controlled afghanistan because  the u.s can reach us there that will  make it more difficult for them to  rebound so that is a good thing that is  the main benefit  of this strike over the weekend  michael based on your experience from an  intelligence standpoint the knowledge  that you have  how is this strike likely carried out  yeah um nate um  three phases to this the first phase is  there's some there's some intelligence  there's some specific intelligence that  takes you to  um the site that took you to this this  building this mansion in kabul and then  once you're there  you spend a lot of time  um looking at it staring at it watching  the pattern of life  and you're trying to do two things  you're trying to make sure that the  person who you're actually looking at is  the person you think it is so is this  i'm in zawahiri  and then secondly you're looking for  opportunities to conduct a strike where  you don't take out any civilian  casualties and so you spend a lot of  time in phase two phase three then is  actually taking the strike when you're  given that opportunity  and uh michael you know they didn't  touch his family they didn't take his  family out in any of this it was just as  wahiri but he obviously had influence on  i'm sure younger people and his family  people who knew him um when you look at  that what's next and even though i know  you said their reach isn't that broad in  terms of al qaeda  is there  anything that we should be worried about  when you look at the big picture uh and  the influence that al zwari did have all  of these years since 9 11.  so i think it it it we'll see who  replaces him right it could be somebody  younger it could be somebody more  dynamic  certainly somebody who was influenced by  him  he was one of the most violent members  of al-qaeda when his egyptian group  merged with al-qaeda years and years ago  al-qaeda became more violent so to the  extent he's mentored the next leader you  know that that will tell you something  about that person  so i think that's the most important  thing to keep in mind is who replaces  him  what does that relationship with with  the taliban look like and how aggressive  will we be and will will we maintain our  focus on afghanistan and so far we have  and i hope we continue  michael royal thank you  you're welcome
 

